  {
   "id": "stokes-circulation",
   "title": "Local curl, outer circulation",
   "caption": "For \\(v(x,y)=(-y,x)\\), the scalar curl is \\(2\\). Every unit square contributes \\(2\\) to circulation. A shared edge is traversed in opposite directions, so its two integrals cancel. Only the outer boundary remains: \\(0+4+4+0=8\\). Arrows mark integration directions; the circled dot is the normal pointing out of the page. The fading pairs show cancellation in the sum. Rotating the field clockwise to \\(F=(x,y)\\) gives the same value as outward flux, since \\(\\operatorname{div}F=2\\). The affine edge integrals illustrate Green–Stokes and the divergence theorem; a CAS proof of these calculations must still be joined to the theorem's regularity and orientation hypotheses.",
   "alt": "Two views of the square [0,2] by [0,2] for v(x,y)=(-y,x), whose curl is 2. On the left, four unit squares each carry the label 2 and counterclockwise arrows. Paired interior arrows point in opposite directions. On the right, the outer boundary is counterclockwise: bottom and left contribute 0, right and top contribute 4. Both totals are 8. A circled dot denotes the outward-from-page normal.",
