  {
   "id": "mollification",
   "title": "Smoothing by convolution",
   "caption": "Left: scale the compactly supported bump \\(b(x)=\\exp(1-1/(1-x^2))\\) for \\(|x|<1\\), zero outside, to radius \\(\\varepsilon\\), then divide by its integral. Each kernel \\(\\eta_\\varepsilon\\) has mass one: a narrower bump must be taller. Right: a function with four jumps is convolved with that kernel at \\(\\varepsilon=0.5,0.3,0.18,0.08\\). Every plotted value is a numerical integral. The convolution is smooth for each \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and remains between the smallest and largest input values. It agrees exactly with the input wherever that input is constant on the whole interval of length \\(2\\varepsilon\\) seen by the kernel. Smoothness of the input alone would not imply this agreement. The animation holds each computed width, while the first frame already shows all three properties.",
   "alt": "Two panels. Left, three bell-shaped bumps of equal area and different widths, the narrowest being the tallest. Right, a step function with four jumps drawn dashed, and a smooth curve through it that tightens onto the steps as the mollifier width shrinks from 0.5 to 0.08.",
