  {
   "id": "sobolev-ladder",
   "title": "The embedding diagram",
   "caption": "On a bounded smooth domain \\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\), place a Sobolev space with \\(s\\) derivatives in \\(L^p\\) at \\((1/p,s)\\): integrability across, differentiability up. Sobolev's relation \\(1/q=1/p-1/3\\) gives \\(H^1\\hookrightarrow L^6\\). Applying it to a function and its first derivatives gives \\(H^2\\hookrightarrow W^{1,6}\\); Morrey then gives \\(C^{0,1/2}\\), since \\(1-3/6=1/2\\). The shaded region \\(s>3/p\\) marks continuity. The excess \\(s-3/p\\) is a Hölder exponent for the function when strictly between zero and one; higher regularity needs derivative spaces and endpoint qualifications. The dashed Rellich arrow \\(H^1\\hookrightarrow L^2\\) is compact: bounded sequences have strongly convergent subsequences in \\(L^2\\). Boundedness does not force the whole sequence to converge. The domain assumptions supply the boundary regularity needed here.",
   "alt": "An embedding diagram with horizontal coordinate 1/p and vertical coordinate s, on a bounded smooth three-dimensional domain. Solid arrows run from H¹ to L⁶ and from H² through first-order Sobolev regularity with exponent 6 to Hölder regularity with exponent 1/2. A dashed vertical arrow H¹ to L² denotes compact embedding and convergence of a subsequence. The region s > 3/p is shaded for continuity.",
