  {
   "id": "gagliardo-boundary-energy",
   "title": "The Gagliardo energy of a jump",
   "caption": "The zero extension of \\(u=1\\) on \\((0,1)\\) to the real line. Left: in the \\((x,y)\\) plane the difference \\(|u(x)-u(y)|\\) is one on the shaded strips, where exactly one of \\(x,y\\) lies in \\((0,1)\\), and zero elsewhere. The kernel \\(|x-y|^{-1-2s}\\) is singular on the dashed diagonal, which the strips touch only at the corners \\((0,0)\\) and \\((1,1)\\). Right: the energy \\(E_s(\\delta)=\\frac2s\\int_\\delta^1x^{-2s}\\,dx\\) of the strips after removing points \\(x\\) within \\(\\delta\\) of an endpoint, against \\(\\log_{10}(1/\\delta)\\). For \\(s<1/2\\) it increases to the dashed limit \\(2/(s(1-2s))\\); at \\(s=1/2\\) it grows like \\(4\\log(1/\\delta)\\), and for \\(s>1/2\\) like a power of \\(1/\\delta\\).",
   "alt": "Left, a square grid picture: the unit square in the middle, four shaded strips leaving its sides horizontally and vertically, and a dashed diagonal through two corners of the square. Right, four increasing curves against the logarithm of one over delta: two level off at dashed horizontal lines, one rises linearly, and one rises steeply out of the top of the panel.",
