  {
   "id": "mean-value",
   "title": "The average over a sphere is the value at the centre",
   "caption": "Left: \\(u=\\operatorname{Re}(z^3)=x^3-3xy^2\\) on the unit disc. Its zero set consists of the lines at \\(30^\\circ,90^\\circ,150^\\circ\\); the remaining level curves are traced numerically. Both circles have centre \\(c=(0.4,0.05)\\). Right: the values on the circle of radius \\(0.5\\) range from approximately \\(-0.315\\) to \\(0.726\\). The signed departures above and below the dashed line balance: their computed integrals agree within \\(10^{-6}\\). The average is \\(0.061=u(c)\\), also the average on the smaller circle of radius \\(0.25\\). Harmonicity proves this equality for every positive radius whose closed disc stays in the domain. The two numerical averages illustrate that theorem.",
   "alt": "A disc with the level curves of a harmonic function and two circles drawn inside it around a common centre; beside it, the values of the function along the larger circle as a function of angle, oscillating around a dashed horizontal line, with the areas above and below the line shaded and equal.",
