  {
   "id": "lax-milgram-projection",
   "title": "Lax-Milgram as a projection, and Galerkin approximation",
   "caption": "Left: a coercive symmetric form \\(B(v,w)=v^{\\mathsf T}Aw\\) on the plane, \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0.8\\\\0.8&1\\end{pmatrix}\\), and a load \\(f\\). The ellipses are level sets of the energy \\(J(v)=\\tfrac12B(v,v)-f(v)\\), centred at its minimizer, the solution \\(u\\) of \\(B(u,v)=f(v)\\). On the line \\(V_1\\) the Galerkin solution \\(u_1\\) solves \\(B(u_1,v)=f(v)\\) for \\(v\\in V_1\\) only. It is the point where a level set touches the line, and its error \\(u-u_1\\), dashed, is \\(B\\)-orthogonal to \\(V_1\\). The Euclidean foot of the perpendicular \\(\\Pi u\\), hollow, is a different point: Galerkin projects in the geometry of \\(B\\), not of the ambient inner product. Right: \\(-((1+x)u')'=f\\) on \\((0,1)\\) with \\(u=\\sin\\pi x\\), solved with piecewise linear elements on the nested meshes \\(h=1/N\\). The animation refines \\(N=4,8,16\\); the static frame is \\(N=4\\). Below, the error \\(\\|u'-u_h'\\|_{L^2}\\) against \\(h\\) on logarithmic axes, the interpolation error \\(\\|u'-(I_hu)'\\|_{L^2}\\), and the dashed bound of Cea's lemma, \\(M/c=2\\) times the interpolation error, where \\(1\\le 1+x\\le 2\\). Both errors fall like \\(h\\). Cea's lemma is the classical statement; the figure computes it in floating point, and it is not a PDEAtlas theorem.",
   "alt": "Left, a coordinate plane with nested tilted ellipses around a marked point u, a line through the origin, the point u1 where the largest ellipse touches the line, a dashed segment from u to u1, and a different hollow point on the line where the perpendicular from u lands. Right top, a sine arch with a piecewise linear approximation that refines. Right bottom, two rows of error marks falling along parallel lines on logarithmic axes, under a dashed bound line.",
