  {
   "id": "heat-kernel",
   "title": "The heat kernel, and where the maximum principle lives",
   "caption": "Left: \\(\\Gamma(x,t)=\\exp(-x^2/(4t))/\\sqrt{4\\pi t}\\) at six positive times. The active curve steps through the computed snapshots while faint curves retain the others. The peak falls and the width grows, but the exact mass on \\(\\mathbb R\\) stays one. Quadrature on \\([-12,12]\\), wider than the plotted window, agrees with this mass to the displayed precision. Since \\(\\Gamma(x,t)>0\\) for \\(t>0\\), convolution with nonnegative initial data of positive mass is immediately positive everywhere. Right: for a homogeneous heat solution continuous on the closed bounded cylinder, the maximum is controlled by the initial slice and spatial sides. The final slice \\(t=T\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\times(0,T]\\), but is not part of the parabolic boundary where comparison data are supplied.",
