  {
   "id": "heat-positivity",
   "title": "Heat reaches every point at once",
   "caption": "The rod \\(0<x<\\pi\\) has ends held at zero. The initial temperature \\(g\\) (dashed) is a smooth bump supported in \\([1.95,2.65]\\). Left: the solution at five times, computed by the method of images and checked against the sine series. Right: the value at \\(x=0.6\\), which lies outside the support of \\(g\\), on a logarithmic scale. It is positive at every positive time, of size about \\(e^{-d^2/4t}\\), where \\(d=1.35\\) is the distance to the support. By the strong principle a nonnegative solution that vanished at an interior point at a time \\(t>0\\) would vanish identically before \\(t\\).",
   "alt": "Left, a rod profile: a narrow dashed bump near x = 2.3 and the solution at five times, spreading and flattening while it stays zero at both ends. A dotted vertical line marks x = 0.6. Right, the base-ten logarithm of the solution at x = 0.6 against time: it rises from below ten to the minus twenty at very small times towards ten to the minus one. The static frame shows time 0.03.",
