  {
   "id": "galerkin-sine-convergence",
   "title": "Galerkin approximations in sine modes",
   "caption": "On \\((0,\\pi)\\) the sines are orthogonal both in \\(L^2\\) and in \\(H^1_0\\), so each Gram threshold space is spanned by the first \\(N\\) of them. The Galerkin solution for \\(u_t=u_{xx}\\) with initial value the step \\(g=\\mathbf 1_{(\\pi/4,3\\pi/4)}\\) is then the truncated series \\(u_N=\\sum_{k\\le N}\\hat g_k e^{-k^2t}\\sin kx\\). Left: \\(u_N\\) at \\(t=0\\) (thin) and at \\(t=0.02\\) (thick) for \\(N=1,3,5,9,17,33\\); the step is dashed. Right: the \\(L^2\\) error \\(\\|u(t)-u_N(t)\\|_2\\) against \\(N\\) on a logarithmic scale. At \\(t=0\\) it decays only like \\(N^{-1/2}\\). At \\(t=0.02\\) the factor \\(e^{-k^2t}\\) makes it fall faster than any power of \\(N\\). The even coefficients of this step vanish, so the errors come in equal pairs.",
   "alt": "Left, a dashed step on the interval with two approximating curves: an oscillating truncated sine series at time zero and a smooth curve at time 0.02, both changing as the number of modes increases; the static frame shows nine modes. Right, two sequences of dots against the number of modes on a logarithmic error axis: the time-zero errors decrease slowly, the time-0.02 errors drop steeply below ten to the minus six.",
