  {
   "id": "dalembert",
   "title": "One shape, two halves, one cone",
   "caption": "Left: the unit-speed wave equation with initial displacement \\(g\\) and zero initial velocity has \\(u(x,t)=\\tfrac12g(x+t)+\\tfrac12g(x-t)\\). Here \\(g\\) is a smooth bump supported on \\([-0.5,0.5]\\). At \\(t=0\\), the coincident halves are dashed at half height beneath their sum; they then separate without changing shape. Right: the backward characteristic cone from \\((x,t)=(0.55,1.15)\\) has base \\([-0.6,1.7]\\). The displacement contributes its two endpoint values. Initial velocity \\(h\\) would add \\(\\tfrac12\\int_{x-t}^{x+t}h(y)\\,dy\\). Changing either datum outside that interval cannot change the solution at the apex: d'Alembert's formula makes finite propagation explicit.",
   "alt": "Three snapshots of a smooth bump splitting into two half-height copies moving left and right at speed one. Only at time zero are the coincident halves dashed beneath their solid sum. A neighbouring space-time plot shows a backward cone with slopes plus and minus one and a shaded base interval of dependence.",
