  {
   "id": "cone-energy",
   "title": "Energy in a backward cone",
   "caption": "The one-dimensional wave with a smooth bump as displacement and zero velocity. Left: the energy density \\(e=\\tfrac12(u_t^2+u_x^2)\\) is carried by the two half-waves along \\(x\\pm t=\\text{const}\\), shaded. The backward cone has apex \\((1,2.2)\\) and base \\([-1.2,3.2]\\), which contains the support of the data. Right: the energy \\(E(t)=\\int_{|x-1|<2.2-t}e\\,dx\\), computed by quadrature. It starts at the total energy and falls in two stages, as the left half crosses the rising edge near \\(t=0.5\\) and the right half crosses the falling edge near \\(t=1.7\\); each stage has two parts because the density of a half-wave, \\(\\tfrac14g'^2\\), vanishes where \\(g'\\) does. It never rises: the edges move at the wave speed, so energy can leave the cone but not enter it, and zero data on the base would keep it zero.",
   "alt": "Two panels. Left, the space-time plane with two shaded diagonal strips leaving a bump at time zero, one to each side, and a triangle whose edges have slopes plus and minus one; each strip is darker where it lies inside the triangle. Right, the energy inside the triangle against time, a curve that starts at the total energy, drops to half near time one half and to zero near time 1.7.",
