  {
   "id": "entropy-selection",
   "title": "What vanishing viscosity selects",
   "caption": "Exact solutions of \\(u_t+uu_x=\\varepsilon u_{xx}\\) with Riemann data, by the Cole-Hopf transformation, at \\(t=1\\) for \\(\\varepsilon=0.16,0.04,0.01\\) (the accent curve is the smallest). Left: \\(u_L=0<u_R=1\\). The expansion shock with a jump at \\(x=1/2\\), dashed, is a weak solution of the inviscid equation, but the viscous solutions converge to the fan \\(u=x/t\\) instead. Right: \\(u_L=1>u_R=0\\). The viscous solutions are smooth fronts of width about \\(\\varepsilon\\) travelling at the Rankine-Hugoniot speed \\(1/2\\), and they converge to the shock. In both cases the \\(L^1\\) distance to the limit at \\(t=1\\) falls with \\(\\varepsilon\\). The limits are the two branches of the entropy Riemann solution.",
   "alt": "Two panels of profiles against x. Left, three smooth increasing curves from zero to one that approach a straight ramp from x = 0 to x = 1, while a dashed vertical jump at x = one half is not approached. Right, three smooth decreasing fronts from one to zero that steepen toward a vertical jump at x = one half.",
