   "caption": "Burgers' equation with initial data \\(g(y)=\\sin\\pi(y-\\tfrac12)\\) on \\([-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12]\\) and \\(1.6\\sin\\pi(y-\\tfrac12)\\) on \\([\\tfrac12,\\tfrac32]\\), solved by the Lax-Oleinik formula: \\(u=(x-y^*)/t\\) with \\(y^*\\) minimizing \\((x-y)^2/(2t)+\\int_0^yg\\). The lobe masses are \\(p=4/\\pi\\) and \\(q=6.4/\\pi\\). Left: \\(\\sqrt t\\,u\\) against \\(x/\\sqrt t\\) at \\(t=1,4,16,64\\), the accent curve being the latest. In these variables the N-wave, which equals \\(x/t\\) for \\(-\\sqrt{pt}<x<\\sqrt{qt}\\) and zero outside, is the fixed shape drawn thin in ink, with jumps at \\(-\\sqrt p\\) and \\(\\sqrt q\\). Right: the computed \\(\\int|u-N|\\,dx\\) against \\(t\\) on logarithmic axes, with a dashed line of slope \\(-1/2\\); the last steps have slopes between -0.5 and -0.49. The rate is attained here because \\(\\int_0^yg\\) is smallest at \\(y=\\tfrac12\\), so the footpoints tend to \\(\\tfrac12\\) and not to the centre of the N-wave.",
