   "id": "taylor-green-vortex",
   "title": "Cells that keep their shape while they decay",
   "caption": "Left: streamlines of the planar Taylor-Green vortex over one period square. The streamfunction is \\(\\psi=e^{-2t}\\sin x\\sin y\\), so \\(u=e^{-2t}\\sin x\\cos y\\) and \\(v=-e^{-2t}\\cos x\\sin y\\). The vorticity \\(\\omega=2\\psi\\) is positive in the shaded cells, which turn counterclockwise, and negative in the others. Because \\(\\omega\\) is a multiple of \\(\\psi\\), advection carries vorticity along its own level sets and the nonlinear term is a pressure gradient. Only the viscous term is left, and at unit viscosity it removes amplitude at the rate \\(|k|^2=2\\). The drawn curves are the levels \\(\\sin x\\sin y=\\pm0.2,\\pm0.5,\\pm0.8\\); they are the same at every time. Right: the kinetic energy in the period square is \\(\\pi^2e^{-4t}\\). The dots are a midpoint quadrature of that energy at seven times, divided by its value at \\(t=0\\). On the whole plane the flow has infinite energy.",
   "alt": "Left: a square of side two pi divided into four cells of closed streamlines, two shaded cells turning counterclockwise and two unshaded cells turning clockwise, with arrow heads on one streamline of each cell. Right: two decaying exponential curves, the amplitude exp(-2t) and the energy ratio exp(-4t), with dots on the energy curve.",
