  {
   "id": "axisymmetric-meridional-flow",
   "title": "Squeeze the radius, stretch the height",
   "caption": "Take the smooth reduced streamfunction \\(\\Psi(s,z)=z\\), with \\(s=r^2\\) and zero swirl. The meridional velocity is \\((-r,2z)\\). Dots follow \\(r(t)=r_0e^{-t}\\) and \\(z(t)=z_0e^{2t}\\); the curves trace their paths. Revolving the cross-section about the axis gives the material cylinder at right. Its radius \\(R\\) shrinks and its half-height \\(H\\) grows, preserving the volume \\(2\\pi R^2H\\). Meridional area alone is not volume: the three-dimensional measure carries the radial factor \\(2\\pi r\\). Faint outlines retain the initial and final cylinders when motion is paused. The three Cartesian stretch factors are \\(e^{-t},e^{-t},e^{2t}\\), whose product is \\(\\det D\\Phi_t=1\\). This linear flow illustrates incompressibility; it is not the manuscript's profile.",
   "alt": "Left: the r,z half-plane, with the symmetry axis vertical. Six particles move inward and away from z=0 along curves r²z=constant. Right: an axisymmetric cylinder becomes narrower and taller. Faint outlines show t=0 and t=0.8. With radius R and half-height H, its volume 2πR²H does not change. The animation moves forward, holds, and restarts; the streamlines and both outlines remain visible.",
