  {
   "id": "stability-cost",
   "title": "Why a smaller bootstrap radius is not always better",
   "caption": "Suppose the perturbation energy obeys \\(\\tfrac12\\,d(E^2)/ds\\le-\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{stab}}E^2+\\Lambda_3E^3+\\Lambda_R E\\). At a positive boundary \\(E=\\delta\\), divide by \\(\\delta^2\\): damping must exceed the combined cost \\(\\Lambda_3\\delta+\\Lambda_R/\\delta\\). The rising line is nonlinear work; the falling dashed curve is the relative cost of a fixed reference residual. Their sum has a minimum at \\(\\delta=\\sqrt{\\Lambda_R/\\Lambda_3}\\). This teaching example uses \\(\\Lambda_3=1\\), \\(\\Lambda_R=0.04\\), and \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{stab}}=0.5\\), so the minimum is \\(0.4\\) at \\(\\delta=0.2\\), and the strict inward-pointing inequality holds exactly for \\(0.1<\\delta<0.4\\). Equality at the open endpoints does not give a strict margin. With initial energy below such a radius, continuity and a valid energy estimate exclude a first crossing. Every other hypothesis, including radius-dependent estimates and the interval of existence, still applies. These coefficients are illustrative; they are not certified profile bounds for the De Gregorio application.",
   "alt": "The combined cost delta plus 0.04/delta is U-shaped. It falls below the horizontal damping threshold 0.5 between the open endpoints delta=0.1 and delta=0.4. The minimum is 0.4 at delta=0.2. Separate rising and falling curves show the nonlinear and residual contributions. All coefficients are explicitly labelled as teaching values.",
