  {
   "id": "interval-enclosure",
   "title": "What interval arithmetic overestimates, and what subdivision buys",
   "caption": "The function \\(f(x)=x/(1+x^2)\\) on \\([-2,2]\\) has exact range \\([-1/2,1/2]\\), attained at \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=1\\). Left: one interval evaluation gives \\([0,4]\\) for the square, \\([1,5]\\) for the denominator, and \\([-2,2]\\) for the quotient, four times the height of the true range. The slack comes from treating the two occurrences of \\(x\\) independently. Right: subdivide the input, evaluate each piece, and take the union. At four, eight, sixteen, and thirty-two pieces, the enclosing hull tightens from \\([-1,1]\\) to \\([-0.567,0.567]\\), always containing the dashed range. These interval operations use exact rational arithmetic; printed decimal endpoints are rounded outwards. Subdivision reduces dependency error here at the cost of more evaluations. A checked CAS enclosure additionally supplies a proof connecting the arithmetic bounds to every value of the original expression on its domain.",
   "alt": "Two panels at the same scale. Left, one tall shaded box containing the whole graph of a small function, showing how loose a single interval evaluation is. Right, the same curve under a row of much shorter boxes that refine in four steps, with the enclosing interval printed and shrinking toward two dashed lines that mark the true range.",
