   "id": "certificate-budget",
   "title": "The error budget, and the case where it closes",
   "caption": "The solid curve is \\(e^x\\); the dashed curve is its Taylor polynomial \\(p\\) at zero, of degree one, two, or three. The band is \\(p\\pm\\varepsilon\\) on \\([0,1]\\). The error \\(e^x-p(x)\\) is nonnegative and increasing there, so its maximum is \\(\\varepsilon=e-p(1)\\). The animation holds each degree before replacing \\(p\\) by the exponential itself, when \\(\\varepsilon=0\\). This illustrates the uniform inequality carried by ApproxOn. The plotted coordinates and decimal labels are numerical illustrations; a CAS certificate must prove the inequality throughout the interval.",
   "alt": "A plot of the exponential on the unit interval with a Taylor polynomial inside a shaded tube of half-width epsilon. The tube shrinks from about 0.718 to 0.218 to 0.052 as the degree rises, and closes to zero width when the approximation is replaced by the function itself.",
