   "title": "A Taylor polynomial with a uniform remainder",
   "caption": "Left: \\(f(x)=x/(1+x^2)\\) and \\(p_5(x)=x-x^3+x^5\\). On the shaded domain \\([-0.6,0.6]\\), the narrow tube \\(p_5\\pm\\rho\\) encloses \\(f\\). At \\(x=1\\), the polynomial equals one while \\(f(1)=1/2\\); this model makes no error claim outside its domain. Right: the signed remainder has its own vertical scale. Multiplication gives \\((1+x^2)p_5=x+x^7\\), so \\(f-p_5=-x^7/(1+x^2)\\). Its absolute value increases with \\(|x|\\); the sharp uniform radius is \\(\\rho=(0.6)^7/(1+0.6^2)=2187/106250\\), approximately \\(0.021\\). The remainder is positive at the left endpoint and negative at the right. A CAS can prove the rational identity; an enclosure also needs the domain-wide inequality \\(|f-p_5|\\le\\rho\\). The generator's numerical spot checks do not supply that proof.",
   "alt": "Left, a rational function and its dashed Taylor polynomial nearly coincide on a shaded central domain but separate outside it. A narrow tube around the polynomial encloses the function on that domain. Right, the odd signed remainder decreases from plus rho at x = −0.6 to minus rho at x = 0.6, flattening near zero; dashed horizontal lines mark the sharp positive and negative bounds.",
