  {
   "id": "gauss-exactness-limit",
   "title": "Degree five is exact, degree six is not",
   "caption": "The three-point Gauss-Legendre nodes are the roots \\(0,\\pm\\sqrt{3/5}\\) of the monic Legendre polynomial \\(p_3(x)=x^3-\\tfrac35x\\). Left: \\(p_3(x)\\,x\\) has degree four and vanishes at every node, so the rule returns zero; orthogonality makes the signed area zero as well, and the rule is right. Every polynomial of degree at most five splits as \\(p_3q+r\\) with \\(q,r\\) of degree at most two, so this is the whole argument for exactness. Right: \\(p_3^2\\) has degree six and also vanishes at the nodes, but its area is \\(8/175>0\\). No rule with three nodes integrates it, and the same area is the constant in the error formula.",
   "alt": "Two panels over the interval from minus one to one, with three dots on the axis at the nodes. Left, a quartic curve with two shaded lobes above the axis and two below, labelled integral zero. Right, a nonnegative sextic curve touching the axis at the three nodes, with its shaded area labelled eight over 175.",
   "width": "640",
   "height": "282",
   "animated": "",
   "marks": [
    {
     "k": "text",
     "x": "24",
     "y": "36",
