  {
   "id": "positive-rule-convergence",
   "title": "Positive weights converge, Newton-Cotes need not",
   "caption": "Left: the error of the closed Newton-Cotes rule and of the Gauss-Legendre rule with \\(n\\) points for \\(\\int_{-1}^1 dx/(1+25x^2)=\\tfrac25\\arctan 5\\), on a logarithmic scale. The Newton-Cotes values are exact rational numbers, so their growth is not rounding. Right: the sum of the absolute weights. For a rule exact on constants with nonnegative weights this sum is the length \\(2\\), which is what the Polya-Steklov bound uses. Newton-Cotes weights turn negative at \\(n=9\\) and from \\(n=11\\) on, and their absolute sum grows without bound.",
   "alt": "Two panels over the number of points n from 2 to 24. Left, logarithmic error: the Newton-Cotes points oscillate and then climb above one, while the Gauss points fall steadily to about ten to the minus four and a half. Right, the sum of absolute weights: Gauss stays on the line at two, Newton-Cotes rises in a zigzag to above a thousand.",
